Added todo #19: Smallest safe decomposition. One code-writing delegate should own the paired store/test changes after architect approval; the parent keeps branch/PR, verification, review, and merge-gate ownership."}],"details":{"action":"add","todos":[{"id":1,"text":"`how` over the affected subsystem.","done":false},{"id":2,"text":"`architect` for parallel design exploration.","done":false},{"id":3,"text":"Write the throughput checkpoint as four todo items. A dimension that genuinely does not apply (single file, no fan-out) keeps its item with `n/a: <reason>` rather than being dropped:
   - **Blocking first steps.** Gates run before fan-out.
   - **Independent workstreams.** Disjoint files, services, or layers parallelize. Shared writes serialize.
   - **Shared mutable state.** Default to splitting the target (the **separate-before-serializing-shared-state** principle skill). Serialize only for real invariants.
   - **Smallest safe decomposition.** If one worker is best, name why.","done":false},{"id":4,"text":"Delegate code-writing to a subagent using your configured feature model (default `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5:xhigh`) with a specific scope (file paths, named data shape and its organizing structure per **principle-model-the-domain**, a state machine over scattered booleans, a table/registry over branching, a typed model over repeated shape assumptions, chosen before the delegate writes logic, and success criteria). When the implementation admits multiple valid shapes (error handling, abstraction layer, test structure), delegate via the **arena** skill instead so the runners surface the alternatives and the cross-judge guards the pick. Mandatory: no skip-with-reason escape, and Laziness Protocol does not override it (the gain is review separation, not lines saved). A subagent forbidden to spawn satisfies this by owning the diff directly with the same review separation. No "standing by" reply that waits on a nested agent. Comments per **Comments**. Surgical edits, re-ground against the source for upstream-derived files. Port shared-primitive improvements to all consumers and verify each. Commit liberally.","done":false},{"id":5,"text":"Verify on the matching surface. "Inconclusive" or wrong-surface is not a pass. Flag it.","done":false},{"id":6,"text":"Rebase into small, ordered commits. Stack follow-ups.
   Use the **sequence-verifiable-units** principle skill, building, verifying, and committing each small unit before the next.","done":false},{"id":7,"text":"If the design is contested, `interrogate` before shipping.","done":false},{"id":8,"text":"Run **Opening a PR**.","done":false},{"id":9,"text":"**Mark the operator's items and honor state-then-wait.** Items the operator names stay with the operator. The operator reviews and clicks, and no owner merges one. An operator-named item is one the operator reserves for themselves, such as "I'll merge #12 myself" or "leave the auth PR to me". The work the operator hands the autopilot, including every item in the request that starts it, is the queue, and its owners merge on a clean verdict. When the operator asks for the protocol or the plan to be stated, deliver the statement and stop. Execution starts only on the operator's explicit go. On that go, arm a `/goal` with the full program objective using `CreateGoal({objective: "<full program objective>"})`.","done":false},{"id":10,"text":"**Spawn one owner per PR with the full lifecycle and an early trail.** Resolve the forge once for the program. GitHub CLI (`gh`) is the default. If `command -v origin` succeeds and Origin can resolve the repository, use `origin pr ...` for PR create, edit, view, watch, and merge operations. Otherwise stay on `gh` and record the fallback. Never require Graphite (`gt`). Cloud Tasks need a clean source checkout and get a managed local worktree. Within about 15 minutes, every owner starts a `decisions.tsv` trail per the **show-me-your-work** skill, pushes its first branch snapshot, and opens the PR ready, never draft. Open the PR before self-proof so the URL, decisions, and checks form a durable trail. Keep `decisions.tsv` uncommitted and return it with the reports. As soon as a Task returns an `agent_id`, the owner adds that id, the expected runtime (at least the longest past run of that kind), and the state to a `children.tsv` kept the same way. The owner does the first rebase before the code-ready report and babysit, whether or not trunk has drifted. In fix rounds, the owner keeps that merge base. The owner rebases again only at merge prep (step 5), on a `git merge-tree` conflict with trunk, or on a CI failure that comes from a change on trunk. When the shipped code is final, after the slop-strip and `/skill:no-comments`, it reports the code-ready head SHA. It also reports the SHA of each later push that changes the patch. Self-proof, CI, and babysit then run in parallel with the swarm. The owner reports merge-ready with the head SHA when self-proof, CI, and babysit finish. Before a push that starts a round, run the pre-review checks that the repo's AGENTS.md files and rules name for the touched paths. Run them on the committed head. A hook pass is not proof. To publish each rebase, check the remote with `git ls-remote`, then push the owner's own branch with `git push --force-with-lease`. Never force-push a shared branch. The merge is the one step an owner may not take alone. Step 4 gates it.","done":false},{"id":11,"text":"**Run owners in true parallel and never stack.** Many owners at once when PRs are self-contained: one writer per branch, disjoint files, cross-PR drift absorbed by rebase. Only genuinely overlapping work serializes. Self-contained PRs branch straight off main, and sequenced work is merge-then-branch. One exception: an owner that must split a genuinely dependent change may hold a short private base-branch stack.","done":false},{"id":12,"text":"**Swarm-verify every round before its merge.** A round starts at the owner's code-ready head SHA and at each later push that changes the PR's patch. At that SHA, fan out parallel independent verifiers per the **swarm** skill and aggregate to one verdict. The merge needs a clean verdict from the round whose patch matches the merge-ready head. Audit the receipts in the merge-ready report before the verdict. The lanes: re-run the gates at that SHA. Prove the load-bearing behavior live on the real surface the change touches (with the matching control skill, such as `control-cli` or `control-ui`, or a named driver where none exists). Audit the diff, distrusting the PR body. Run the audit as two or more review lanes with the full brief. Give each lane one main focus, such as consumer parity with trunk, lifetimes and races, or data and config safety. **Regression lane against trunk.** Run the same load-bearing scenario on current trunk. If trunk does not have the feature, record that fact and gate the behavior the diff adds plus the end state the user waits for instead of pretending trunk can produce it. The live lane is the floor, and a verdict without it is not clean.","done":false},{"id":13,"text":"**On a clean verdict the owner merges and takes the next item.** The owner merges only from a head freshly rebased onto trunk. Merge prep never comes before a round's lanes start, and it ends with a rebase onto current trunk right before the merge. CI must pass on that head before the merge, and the patch-id rule decides whether the round's verdict still holds. After the merge-prep rebase, the owner reports the new head SHA. If trunk moves again before the merge, the patch-id rule in `playbooks/shipping.md` governs re-verification. A new head voids the verdict unless the patch-id is unchanged. The operator's full-autonomy grant plus the root's clean verdict is the merge authorization. The operator's named gate stops at merge-ready until its authorization arrives.","done":false},{"id":14,"text":"**Run the root layer.** A genuinely new raise of a pinned gate or budget value (a limit CI only lets tighten) needs your fresh countersign, granted only after verifier proof. If the operator's grant or standing orders cover approvals, that countersign is the approval. The owner records it in the form that the tool's approval contract allows, with a pointer to the root's countersign. A lane checks the record against that countersign. The root never gives or bypasses an approval that the forge enforces. Run an audit tick over all owners roughly every 30 minutes. A local root arms each tick as a real terminal `/loop`, implemented in Pi as a monitored background `Shell` loop that sleeps 1800 seconds (30 minutes) and emits a unique output-notification sentinel. Set `output_notification` to match it, per `references/pi-runtime.md`. Never leave the cadence to memory or lossy completion notifications. Stop the loop when the queue is done. At each tick, re-read this playbook from trunk with `git show origin/main:pstack/skills/poteto-mode/playbooks/autopilot-full.md`, or from its installed path (`playbooks/autopilot-full.md`), then re-read the armed `/goal`. Audit the operation against both. Fix drift during that tick. Probe each owner with a generic liveness or status check. In Pi, use `SubagentAwait({agent_id, timeout_ms: 0})` and collect the decision trails. Count only side effects as progress: commits, pushes, PR or check deltas, and store reports. Treat a lane that errors, or that passes its expected runtime without a side effect, as stuck. Stand it down and dispatch a replacement at once. Do not wait for a polite return. A stall never proves or drops the work. When merges batch, run a retro pass and a post-merge bot-comment sweep. End the tick only when no delegated work is left, even after the last merge. When the queue is complete and the predicate is proven, call `UpdateGoal({status: "COMPLETE"})` and stop the audit Shell loop.","done":false},{"id":15,"text":"**Stand down instantly on the operator's stop.** The operator's hold or stand-down reaches every owner as a zero-writes order immediately.","done":false},{"id":16,"text":"Blocking first steps. Confirm repo instructions, baseline test/typecheck commands, current model/store semantics, and forge access before review fan-out.","done":false},{"id":17,"text":"Independent workstreams. n/a: the store mutation and its behavior tests are one tightly coupled feature slice; design exploration is read-only and parallel.","done":false},{"id":18,"text":"Shared mutable state. Serialize all code edits to the module-level Todo[] store; architect/review delegates read only until the implementation owner consolidates findings.","done":false},{"id":19,"text":"Smallest safe decomposition. One code-writing delegate should own the paired store/test changes after architect approval; the parent keeps branch/PR, verification, review, and merge-gate ownership.","done":false}],"nextId":20},"isError":false,"timestamp":1790323384016}}

