{"type":"message","id":"2240d69b","parentId":"d2055b13","timestamp":"2026-09-25T10:00:48.731Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7yRyssqpSSrJNe27AGfl9YG4|fc_037b53db53fa1d61016ab64650901087d0bce589d4d6210377","toolName":"todo","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Todo #1 completed"}],"details":{"action":"set","todos":[{"id":1,"text":"Locate the prior trail. A local transcript under the active workspace's `agent-transcripts/` directory (on Pi, the local equivalent is the directory holding `$PI_SESSION_FILE`, per `references/pi-runtime.md`. Do not glob across `${PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR:-$HOME/.pi/agent}/sessions/*/`, that crosses workspace boundaries and reads private chats from unrelated projects), a cloud-agent run's `agent_id`, or a pushed branch. On Pi, a cloud-agent run is a worktree Task; read it with `SubagentAwait({agent_id, timeout_ms: 0})`, then its transcript and artifact paths. Read the metadata overview and last messages first, then scan back for the decision points. Parse a long transcript in a subagent and keep the reduced timeline in the main thread (the **principle-guard-the-context-window** skill).","done":true,"status":"completed"},{"id":2,"text":"Reconstruct operational state. The branch and worktree, what already landed (`git log`, `git diff` against the base), the open todos, the decisions made. The prior trail is authoritative input. Resist the bias to re-derive it.","done":false},{"id":3,"text":"Diff done vs pending. Compare what shipped against what was planned, name the resume point, do not re-run the prior repro or redo completed work. A "let me verify from scratch" pass means you're treating the trail as untrustworthy when it's authoritative.","done":false},{"id":4,"text":"Route the remaining work to the matching playbook and pick the verdict: continue the execution, ship a finished recommendation, ratify or override a prior conclusion, or postmortem a failed run. The pickup playbook ends here. The routed playbook owns the rest.","done":false},{"id":5,"text":"Verify the inherited claims against the original goal on the real artifact (the **prove-it-works** principle). A passing prior self-report is not the proof.","done":false},{"id":6,"text":"Mark the operator's items and honor state-then-wait. Items the operator names stay with the operator. The operator reviews and clicks, and no owner merges one. An operator-named item is one the operator reserves for themselves, such as "I'll merge #12 myself" or "leave the auth PR to me". The work the operator hands the autopilot, including every item in the request that starts it, is the queue, and its owners merge on a clean verdict. When the operator asks for the protocol or the plan to be stated, deliver the statement and stop. Execution starts only on the operator's explicit go. On that go, arm a `/goal` with the full program objective using `CreateGoal({objective: "<full program objective>"})`. The goal continues across turns until the queue is done.","done":false},{"id":7,"text":"Spawn one owner per PR with the full lifecycle and an early trail. Resolve the forge once for the program. GitHub CLI (`gh`) is the default. If `command -v origin` succeeds and Origin can resolve the repository, use `origin pr ...` for PR create, edit, view, watch, and merge operations. Otherwise stay on `gh` and record the fallback. Never require Graphite (`gt`). One background `Task` per PR owns build, the first push, a ready PR, self-proof on the real artifact (the **prove-it-works** principle skill), skeptical Bugbot triage per `references/bugbot-triage.md`, a slop-strip (the **deslop** skill (`/skill:deslop`)), `/skill:no-comments` (the **no-comments** skill), a rebase onto current trunk, the babysit loop to green (`playbooks/babysit.md`), and the merge itself. Launch independent owners with parallel Task calls in one message. Set `description`, `prompt`, `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"`, `run_in_background: true`, `environment: "cloud"`, and `cloud_base_branch: "origin/main"` after `git fetch origin main`. Cloud Tasks need a clean source checkout and get a managed local worktree. Within about 15 minutes, every owner starts a `decisions.tsv` trail per the **show-me-your-work** skill, pushes its first branch snapshot, and opens the PR ready, never draft. Open the PR before self-proof so the URL, decisions, and checks form a durable trail. Keep `decisions.tsv` uncommitted and return it with the reports. As soon as a Task returns an `agent_id`, the owner adds that id, the expected runtime (at least the longest past run of that kind), and the state to a `children.tsv` kept the same way. The owner does the first rebase before the code-ready report and babysit, whether or not trunk has drifted. In fix rounds, the owner keeps that merge base. The owner rebases again only at merge prep (step 5), on a `git merge-tree` conflict with trunk, or on a CI failure that comes from a change on trunk. When the shipped code is final, after the slop-strip and `/skill:no-comments`, it reports the code-ready head SHA. It also reports the SHA of each later push that changes the patch. Self-proof, CI, and babysit then run in parallel with the swarm. The owner reports merge-ready with the head SHA when self-proof, CI, and babysit finish. Before a push that starts a round, run the pre-review checks that the repo's AGENTS.md files and rules name for the touched paths. Run them on the committed head. A hook pass is not proof. To publish each rebase, check the remote with `git ls-remote`, then push the owner's own branch with `git push --force-with-lease`. Never force-push a shared branch. The merge is the one step an owner may not take alone. Step 4 gates it. The root spawns the owners right after the go. Design work (the **how** and **architect** skills) happens inside each owner's build, never in the root before spawning, and comes after the owner's first push and ready PR, so the 15-minute trail exists before any design fan-out.","done":false},{"id":8,"text":"Run the root layer. A genuinely new raise of a pinned gate or budget value (a limit CI only lets tighten) needs your fresh countersign, granted only after verifier proof. If the operator's grant or standing orders cover approvals, that countersign is the approval. The owner records it in the form that the tool's approval contract allows, with a pointer to the root's countersign. A lane checks the record against that countersign. The root never gives or bypasses an approval that the forge enforces. Absorbing values that already landed on main is drift, not a raise. Run an audit tick over all owners roughly every 30 minutes. A local root arms each tick as a real terminal `/loop`, implemented in Pi as a monitored background `Shell` loop that sleeps 1800 seconds (30 minutes) and emits a unique output-notification sentinel. Set `output_notification` to match it, per `references/pi-runtime.md`. Never leave the cadence to memory or lossy completion notifications. Stop the loop when the queue is done. At each tick, re-read this playbook from trunk with `git show origin/main:pstack/skills/poteto-mode/playbooks/autopilot-full.md`, or from its installed path (`playbooks/autopilot-full.md`), then re-read the armed `/goal`. Audit the operation against both. Fix drift during that tick. Probe each owner with a generic liveness or status check. In Pi, use `SubagentAwait({agent_id, timeout_ms: 0})` and collect the decision trails. Count only side effects as progress: commits, pushes, PR or check deltas, and store reports. Treat a lane that errors, or that passes its expected runtime without a side effect, as stuck. Stand it down and dispatch a replacement at once. Do not wait for a polite return. Each tick also runs the lane stuck test over the program's agent list, which in this Pi flow is each owner's `children.tsv`, using `SubagentAwait` status probes. Stop a stuck Task with `Task({resume: <agent_id>, interrupt: true})`. Whether or not a stop works, the root has the owner 